body:
I just hit this as well, on my first day using `'tagfunc'`.  Looking at my core in the `find_tagfunc_tags` frame, I see a definite problem:

```
(gdb) ptype curwin->w_tagstack
type = struct taggy {
    char_u *tagname;
    fmark_T fmark;
    int cur_match;
    int cur_fnum;
    char_u *user_data;
} [20]
(gdb) p curwin->w_tagstackidx
$10 = 20
```

https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/2db0a52074ab6b822edc1fa04eca399d4e727784/src/nvim/tag.c#L1153

So, `tag` is completely bogus at this point.  We're off in someone else's memory.

Looking through the code, I see a couple potential problem areas.

https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/2db0a52074ab6b822edc1fa04eca399d4e727784/src/nvim/tag.c#L681-L683

This should probably be a `tagstackidx < curwin->w_tagstacklen` check -- indexes are 0-based, lengths are not.

https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/2db0a52074ab6b822edc1fa04eca399d4e727784/src/nvim/tag.c#L670-L672

This should probably be `tagstackidx >= curwin->w_tagstacklen`.
